WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:09.117
Laurie burst into Grandpa's study, blowing on a paper noisemaker.

2
00:00:09.118 --> 00:00:13.710
"Grandpa, will you come downstairs to watch the countdown with us?"

3
00:00:13.711 --> 00:00:19.319
It was New Year's Eve, and Grandpa had been in his study since after dinner.

4
00:00:19.320 --> 00:00:24.458
"Of course! Is it that time already?" Grandpa said.

5
00:00:24.459 --> 00:00:34.167
"Yep. It's almost 2011!" Laurie looked at the calendar hanging on the wall. "You need a new calendar!"

6
00:00:34.168 --> 00:00:46.604
"I always find it amazing that humans have been on Earth for well over ten thousand years, but our calendar only says 2011!" Grandpa said.

7
00:00:46.605 --> 00:00:50.693
"What year is it really?" Laurie asked, confused.

8
00:00:50.694 --> 00:00:54.373
"It depends on when you start your calendar.

9
00:00:54.374 --> 00:01:01.602
Most of the world uses a calendar that begins with the birth of Jesus Christ.

10
00:01:01.603 --> 00:01:08.843
Every year before that is marked with 'BC,' which stands for before Christ.

11
00:01:08.844 --> 00:01:18.775
Every year after that is marked by 'AD,' which stands for the Latin words anno Domini , meaning in the year of the

12
00:01:18.776 --> 00:01:25.487
Lord. Before Jesus, most calendars were based on the cycles of the moon or the sun, or

13
00:01:25.488 --> 00:01:32.286
both. It was also common to count years based on the start of a leader's or dynasty's

14
00:01:32.287 --> 00:01:42.569
rule. But Jesus changed the world. In the sixth century, a monk created a calendar that began with the birth of

15
00:01:42.570 --> 00:01:48.720
Jesus." "So the birth of one man changed the way humans record time?" Laurie

16
00:01:48.721 --> 00:01:58.812
asked. "Yes. And not only that. His life and death also gave rise to the largest religion in the

17
00:01:58.813 --> 00:02:03.861
world—Christianity." "I only know a little about Jesus from the stories around Christmas time."

18
00:02:03.862 --> 00:02:09.602
Laurie glanced at the clock. It was 11:46 p.m.

19
00:02:09.603 --> 00:02:12.868
"What was so special about his life?"

20
00:02:12.869 --> 00:02:19.292
"I'll tell you as much as I can—but quickly, because I know Mom and Dad are waiting for us.

21
00:02:19.293 --> 00:02:25.531
Jesus was born in Bethlehem, a Jewish city that was part of the Roman Empire.

22
00:02:25.532 --> 00:02:33.634
He was the son of a carpenter named Joseph and a woman named Mary, who raised him in the Jewish tradition.

23
00:02:33.635 --> 00:02:39.432
Many people believed he was the leader who would free the Jews from Roman rule.

24
00:02:39.433 --> 00:02:43.374
Others thought he was the son of God.

25
00:02:43.375 --> 00:02:48.824
Anyway, Jesus studied Judaism carefully and became a preacher.

26
00:02:48.825 --> 00:02:53.460
He preached about love, heaven, and the forgiveness of sins.

27
00:02:53.461 --> 00:02:58.665
He wanted to tell people everywhere that God loved them.

28
00:02:58.666 --> 00:03:08.462
"Now, although Jesus worshipped the same god as the Jews, his teachings strayed from traditional Jewish beliefs.

29
00:03:08.463 --> 00:03:13.352
So some Jews accepted him, but many others did not.

30
00:03:13.353 --> 00:03:22.085
And the Romans didn't bother him, because they generally allowed their subjects to follow different religions as long as no one harmed the state.

31
00:03:22.086 --> 00:03:30.246
As Jesus gained more and more followers, however, many Jews and Romans began to worry about his power.

32
00:03:30.247 --> 00:03:38.117
When Jesus was just 33 years old, a Roman official ordered him to be killed.

33
00:03:38.118 --> 00:03:46.773
"But even after his death, Jesus' followers kept believing in what he had said, and Christianity arose.

34
00:03:46.774 --> 00:03:54.137
At first this religion was practiced by only a small number of people, and not many others understood it.

35
00:03:54.138 --> 00:04:04.701
But as Jesus' followers journeyed to tell others about their new faith, Christianity spread throughout the Mediterranean world.

36
00:04:04.702 --> 00:04:09.714
"These early Christians weren't treated very well in the Roman Empire.

37
00:04:09.715 --> 00:04:19.113
Roman emperors often held ceremonies related to Roman gods and goddesses. They wanted all their people to participate.

38
00:04:19.114 --> 00:04:25.723
But Christians believed in a single god and wouldn't take part in anything related to Roman gods.

39
00:04:25.724 --> 00:04:33.885
So some Roman leaders saw Christians as a threat because they obeyed their faith rather than the Roman emperor.

40
00:04:33.886 --> 00:04:44.213
These Romans persecuted Christians. But Christianity continued to gain popularity because it gave people hope.

41
00:04:44.214 --> 00:04:50.724
Christians spoke of life after death, when people would live in the kingdom of God.

42
00:04:50.725 --> 00:04:54.951
They also said that all people were equal before God.

43
00:04:54.952 --> 00:05:00.541
This included everyone—from emperors to commoners."

44
00:05:00.542 --> 00:05:07.422
"So I guess common people liked the religion, but the emperors didn't?" Laurie asked.

45
00:05:07.423 --> 00:05:20.250
"Right. Not at first anyway. But in 312 AD, the Roman emperor Constantine converted to Christianity, and persecution of the Christians ended.

46
00:05:20.251 --> 00:05:30.515
In 380 Emperor Theodosius made Christianity the empire's official religion, creating the world's first Christian empire.

47
00:05:30.516 --> 00:05:36.597
Gradually Christians began settling in large communities and building churches.

48
00:05:36.598 --> 00:05:41.453
Bishops, who were the heads of churches, became more powerful.

49
00:05:41.454 --> 00:05:48.327
In big cities, such as Rome, their authority extended from religion into politics.

50
00:05:48.328 --> 00:05:55.677
"Once Christianity gained the approval of Roman emperors, it grew and grew within the empire.

51
00:05:55.678 --> 00:06:06.256
Churches were built using state money. Courts run by churches appeared, and they soon held more power than the Roman government over religious issues. The

52
00:06:06.257 --> 00:06:11.859
bishops of Rome, known as popes, became especially influential. They

53
00:06:11.860 --> 00:06:15.680
claimed authority over all other churches. At

54
00:06:15.681 --> 00:06:23.218
the same time, the Roman Empire was being attacked by nomadic troops from Europe and Asia. As

55
00:06:23.219 --> 00:06:31.992
the state grew weaker and Christianity grew stronger, people found more security with the church than they did with the state. "Soon

56
00:06:31.993 --> 00:06:36.823
Christianity spread beyond the Roman Empire. Part

57
00:06:36.824 --> 00:06:46.242
of Jesus' message was that God was universal, so Christians believed they should spread Jesus' teachings throughout the world. Roman

58
00:06:46.243 --> 00:06:53.731
roads and access to the Mediterranean Sea made it easy for Christians to go on missions. Christians

59
00:06:53.732 --> 00:06:59.069
moved north throughout Europe and converted the people there. By

60
00:06:59.070 --> 00:07:08.696
the time the Roman Empire reached its last days around 500 AD, Christianity had become the dominant religion in Europe. And

61
00:07:08.697 --> 00:07:18.876
that brings us to the eve of the Middle Ages, Laurie, when the Christian church and the pope became the supreme authority on nearly everything." "The

62
00:07:18.877 --> 00:07:32.327
eve of the Middle Ages? Grandpa, it's the eve of 2011 and here we are sitting and talking about stuff that happened over 1,500 years ago! Let's

63
00:07:32.328 --> 00:07:39.504
go downstairs and celebrate what's happening now!" Laurie grabbed Grandpa's hand. "Okay.

64
00:07:39.505 --> 00:07:48.452
You have a point," Grandpa said, standing up. "I should stop rambling." "Happy

65
00:07:48.453 --> 00:07:57.326
2011, anno Domini !" Laurie cried as they walked out of the study together.

